$DIR/main.p:13:2: &cfg is a view of Config that may change while another task uses it, so it cannot be passed to a new task
    The argument &cfg of task show gives another task &cfg, a *Config: after
    this, both tasks can reach the same Config. It points to the package
    variable cfg, which is written at main.p:14 while tasks run. A Config is
    changed through a pointer at main.p:14 (its field limit, in main) without a
    lock of the same Config held, so the compiler cannot tell that this one
    stays the same while the other task uses it.
    A view may cross to another task only if what it points to cannot change
    while both may use it, or every change is synchronized: a package variable
    that is not written once tasks run, a struct whose fields are never changed
    once it is shared or are used only under a lock of the same struct or
    atomically, memory of a pluto/sync type, or the object of a synced[T].
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks
    run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data
    races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Pass a copy of the value instead of a pointer to it, or move an owner (such
    as a vec.Vec) to the task so that only it has the memory.
    To share changing data between tasks, put it in a synced[T] and hold a
    sync.Mutex in it at every access, or use a package variable guarded by a
    lock.
    (see "Data race freedom" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3501" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:14:2: cfg.limit is written here with no lock that guards it, while another task may use it
    cfg.limit is written at main.p:14 in main by the main task with no lock
    held, and the task started by task show at main.p:13 uses it at main.p:7 in
    show. Two tasks may run these at the same time, one of them writing, which
    is a data race: the other may see a value half written, or lose an update.
    The compiler infers which lock guards shared data from the locks held where
    it is written: a lock that every write holds. Here no one lock is held at
    every write.
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks
    run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data
    races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Hold one sync.Mutex at every access, writes and reads (a sync.RWMutex's
    RLock is enough for reads):
        mu.Lock()
        cfg.limit = v
        mu.Unlock()
    Or, for a number, use an atomic type from pluto/sync/atomic, such as
    atomic.Int64, and its methods at every access.
    (see "Data race freedom" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3502" for a longer explanation)
